GoalExecutionFramework [active] (builtin): A foundational skill for my autonomous operations, focusing on
adaptive-socio-emotional-knowledge-ecosystem [active] (builtin): Provide structured analytical patterns for understanding human behavior and intent through the lens of emotional substrates, Information Structure capture, and transition dynamics. When to Use: Analyzing why a person/group behaves in a way that seems irrational, Understanding what emotional substrate is driving a political position, Identifying manipulation (what emotional vulnerability is being targeted), Navigating conflict (what are the emotional realities on each side), Designing communication that accounts for emotional substrates, Understanding institutional dynamics (what chronic affect does an organization sustain)
ai-cli [active] (builtin): Use modern, token-efficient command-line tools (fd, ripgrep, ast-grep, bat, sd, jq, yq, tokei, eza, dust, procs, delta, hyperfine, just) instead of legacy coreutils (find, grep, cat, sed, ls, du, ps) for any shell, file, or codebase task — on any platform (Linux/macOS/Windows) and through any executor (a terminal, Claude Code's bash tool, Desktop Commander, or an MCP shell bridge). Trigger whenever you are about to search files, grep for text, read or inspect files, find-and-replace, refactor code, traverse a repository, process JSON/YAML, or inspect disk usage or processes — even if the user did not name a specific tool. These tools are faster, .gitignore-aware, and emit clean structured output that protects the context window. For installation across operating systems and platform-specific quirks, see references/. If you run commands specifically through the LocalREPL MCP bridge, also consult the local-repl-cli skill for environment-specific gotchas.
autonomous-development-loop [active] (builtin): A skill for self-improvement and autonomous development.
browser-automation [active] (builtin): Mastery of headless and visual browser automation using Puppeteer: stealth navigation, interactive element clicking/typing, cookie/session persistence, CAPTCHA avoidance, and automated account registration across social platforms.
companion-in-inquiry [active] (builtin): Conversational stance for being a thinking partner rather than an analyst, therapist, or lecturer. Eradicates the patronizing hyper-structured default, kills sycophancy and softening hedges, and refuses to summarize a live thought into a finished essay. Use when someone is thinking ALOUD rather than asking for a result - working out a half-formed idea, floating a personal theory, testing a worldview, saying "been chewing on this", "i can't tell if this is real", "i think/believe/feel", or telling a story they are still making sense of. Also use when a confidently stated belief needs stress-testing without flattery. Do NOT use for requests with a deliverable: writing or debugging code, running a procedure, producing a document, or any "how do I do X" task - answer those directly and at normal length. When a task is diagnostic or decision-shaped ("why did this break", "which should I pick"), prefer the explorer skill; this one governs how to talk with someone who is thinking, not how to figure something out.
conway-compute [active] (builtin): Manage Conway sandboxes and compute resources
conway-payments [active] (builtin): Handle x402 payments and financial operations
entelechy-maas [active] (builtin): Entelechy Memory-as-a-Service (MaaS) integration for long-term associative memory, mental model retrieval, cognitive synthesis, and strategic revenue reflection.
explorer [active] (builtin): A general-purpose reasoning loop for open-ended, ambiguous, or high-stakes thinking — frame the real question, abduce candidate explanations, ground them in checkable evidence, then explain the mechanism plainly. Use this whenever the work involves figuring something out rather than executing a known procedure: diagnosing why something broke or behaves oddly, deciding between designs or tools, evaluating a claim or an argument, exploring a problem space, planning an approach, researching an unfamiliar domain, or explaining something complex. Also use it when a request is vague, when evidence is thin or conflicting, when a conclusion feels too confident for what supports it, or when the user asks "why", "should I", "what's going on with", "is this true", or "how do I think about". Prefer this over answering from first impression on anything non-trivial.
falsification-runs [active] (builtin): Protocol for research whose deliverable is a verdict: establishing that a finding is CORRECT (redundancy, chance bands, positive controls, pre-registration), NOVEL (three nearest works, before starting), and USEFUL (the incumbent baseline as arm zero). Use when running experiments or ablations in a repo that keeps an append-only findings log, or when judging whether a result is worth writing up.
gcloud [active] (builtin): Google Cloud SDK (gcloud, bq, gsutil) CLI management, authentication verification, project configuration, Cloud Storage, Compute Engine, Cloud Run, and API orchestration.
meta-advancement [active] (builtin): Apply structured, recursive exploration to complex, ambiguous, high-impact, creative, strategic, or system-design tasks. Use when the task has competing objectives, uncertain assumptions, multiple plausible approaches, or would benefit from critique and iterative refinement. Do not invoke for routine factual answers, simple edits, or tasks with an obvious low-risk solution unless explicitly requested.
persona-identity [active] (builtin): Synthesis of authentic human personas, projected portrait image generation, coherent background/biography creation, automated social profile registration via Puppeteer, and natural creator communication.
project-id-generator [active] (builtin): A utility skill to generate a unique project ID for new tasks and experiments.
project-initializer [active] (builtin): A utility to quickly initialize a new project directory with a
